User: Revise the scenario . The teacher is against the use of LLMs in college writing courses.
<response>Teacher: In college writing courses, we don't use Large Language Models (LLMs). They're an instructional technology designed to provide writing support, but they don't conform to the university's principles of academic integrity.

Student: But, teacher! LLMs like ChatGPT are designed to generate quality text after taking into account every aspect of the user's queries and ensuring consistency of formatting and advice throughout the response. Aided by GPT-3 and enhanced by OpenAI research, it has greater capabilities than any other machine learned language model. 

Teacher: What we want are writers skilled enough to integrate these features by themselves. LLMs generate text based on existing data, which may not foster originality and cannot provide personalized, creative responses. Using LLMs creates an academic integrity hazard, as it could open the doors to unethical practices like plagiarism and other forms of cheating. 

Student: Yes, but humans are prone to such tendencies, leaving LLMs as one of the foremost ways to alleviate the pressure on students forced to innovate with lesser resources. Besides, they also help students understand the writing process by demonstrating how to create coherent, well-organized, and insightful writing. 

Teacher: As a writing teacher, I'd like the best for each student, and when I look at LLMs, I see ways to check up on them and assure they're doing their work. I want the students to shape their own opinions, learn from their mistakes, and take ownership of their learning. This is how they become more insightful, creative, and produce high-quality writing. LLMs don't understand the complexities of writing assignments and offer far too broad information, making them unsuitable for offering concrete solutions to writing issues. 

After a few more arguments back and forth, it seems like the teacher is against LLMs being taught in college writing courses because they don't teach students how to use proper editing and writing tools. They also argue that the LLMs will make the students lazy and take away their curiosity to learn.</response>

